    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The SX5000 is two products sharing one name. The solvent version carries 40% solids and needs a respirator; the water-based WB carries about 20% and cleans up with soap. Foundation Armor gives both the same coverage, the same traffic times and the same up-to-10-year claim, so for most driveways, patios and pool decks the WB in a 5-gallon pail is the one to buy.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="the-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the SX5000 Line Actually Is
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Formulas, One Name</h3>
          <p>
            Foundation Armor sells the SX5000 in two formulas that share a name, a coverage table and a set of claims, and differ in what carries the active ingredient. The original SX5000 is solvent-based. The SX5000 WB is water-based. Both are silane-siloxane water repellents: clear penetrating sealers that soak into concrete, brick, pavers and stone and react inside the pores to make them shed water. Neither leaves a film you can see. Foundation Armor's data sheets describe the dry appearance of both as invisible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizes and Where to Buy</h3>
          <p>
            Both formulas come in the same four sizes, according to Foundation Armor: a 16 oz sample, a 1-gallon bottle, a 5-gallon pail and a 55-gallon drum. Amazon carries the first three, sometimes under several listings for the same product, which is why this review compares exact sizes and part numbers rather than talking about the SX5000 as one thing. The 5-gallon pails carry part numbers SX5000WB5GAL for the water-based version and SX5000LX405GAL for the solvent version, and those are what to look for on a listing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Marketing Claims</h3>
          <p>
            The marketing claims are the same on both data sheets: up to 5 times more active ingredients than similar products, up to 10 years before a recoat, and approval as a water-repellent sealer by the Department of Transportation in several states. Treat the first claim with care. Foundation Armor does not say which similar products it is comparing against, and its own sheets list 40% solids for the solvent SX5000 and about 20% for the WB, so even within the line the active content differs by a factor of two. The 10-year figure is a maximum under good conditions, not a schedule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Independent Test Figures</h3>
          <p>
            The more useful figures on the sheets come from independent test methods. Foundation Armor cites NCHRP Report 244 Series II results of an 81-95% reduction in water absorption and about 84% reduction in chloride ion content. That second number is the reason this kind of sealer is used on bridge decks and road structures: chloride from de-icing salt is what corrodes rebar and breaks up concrete surfaces. For a homeowner, it translates into less spalling and scaling on a driveway that sees road salt carried in on tires.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the background chemistry, our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane guide</a> explains how the two molecules behave and why blends like the SX5000 combine them. If you are still comparing brands, our <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">best penetrating concrete sealer</a> roundup sets the SX5000 WB against Ghostshield, Eagle and silicate densifiers. This page assumes you have settled on Foundation Armor and need to choose the formula and the size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wb-vs-solvent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. SX5000 WB vs Solvent-Based: The Differences That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Side by side, the two data sheets are almost identical. The coverage table is the same, the drying time is the same, the traffic times are the same and the 10-year claim is the same. The differences are in four lines: solids content, recoat timing, clean-up and the safety precautions. Those four lines are enough to decide which to buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
