        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Silane-Siloxane Does, and What It Does Not
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How It Repels Water</h3>
          <p>
            A silane-siloxane sealer makes masonry repel water from the inside. The molecules soak into the pores, react with moisture and the minerals in the concrete, and leave the pore walls water-repellent. Water beads on the surface instead of soaking in, but water vapor can still pass through, which is why Foundation Armor describes the SX5000 as breathable. That matters because trapped moisture under a film sealer is what causes whitening, peeling and delamination.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keeping liquid water out is what protects the concrete. According to Foundation Armor, sealed surfaces resist cracking, spalling, pitting, freeze-thaw and salt damage, efflorescence, and mold and mildew. All of those failures depend on water getting into the concrete and either freezing, carrying salt in, or carrying minerals out. Reduce absorption by 81-95% and you reduce all of them together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Will Not Do</h3>
          <p>
            Now the limits, which Foundation Armor states plainly. The sheets say the sealer is designed as a water repellent for porous surfaces, is not intended as a decorative topcoat, and will not keep water out of cracks, defects or open joints. That last point is important: if water is getting into a basement through a crack or a cold joint, the SX5000 will not fix it. Fill the crack first with a product from our <a href="/best-concrete-crack-filler/">concrete crack filler</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Sheen or Wet Look</h3>
          <p>
            It also adds no sheen. There is no wet look, no color enhancement and no gloss, and the sheets describe the result as maintaining the natural, unsealed look of the surface. If you want stamped concrete or pavers to look darker and richer, you want an acrylic, such as the Foundation Armor sealer covered in our <a href="/armor-ar350-concrete-sealer-review/">Armor AR350 review</a>, not a silane-siloxane.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not an Oil-Stain Barrier</h3>
          <p>
            And it is not an oil-stain barrier. Silane-siloxane chemistry repels water, not oil. Motor oil, grease and many cooking oils will still soak into a sealed driveway or patio, though water-based spills such as wine or soda absorb less and clean up more easily. Foundation Armor's precautions on its siliconate SC25 say outright that it will not stop staining, and the same logic applies to the SX5000. If oil spots are the issue, you need an oil-repellent sealer or regular cleaning; our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers the second option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much SX5000 to Buy
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Published Coverage Rates</h3>
          <p>
            Foundation Armor publishes the same coverage figures for both formulas: 125-175 square feet per gallon in one coat on porous substrates, 250-350 square feet per gallon in one coat on dense, trowel-finished surfaces, and an average of 250. Retailer listings quote wider ranges, from 150 to 250 square feet per gallon. The manufacturer's lower figure for porous concrete is the one to plan with, because most driveways, walks and patios are broom-finished and soak up more than a smooth garage floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Planning for Two Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Most users apply two coats, and both data sheets describe a second coat: wet-on-wet for the WB, after 2 to 24 hours for the solvent version. Plan your purchase on two coats for exterior porous concrete. In practice that means about 60-90 square feet per gallon for the whole job on a rough driveway, and 125-175 on a smooth interior floor.
          </p>

