        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying SX5000 the Way the Data Sheet Says
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Prepare a Clean, Dry Surface</h3>
          <p>
            Most failed silane-siloxane jobs come from the surface, not the sealer. Foundation Armor asks for a clean, dry and absorbent surface and warns that excess moisture inhibits penetration and shortens the life of the treatment. New concrete needs at least four weeks to cure, preferably six. Clean it with a detergent suited to the surface, remove dust with a brush or compressed air, and let a pressure-washed slab dry until there are no damp patches anywhere. Then check the forecast: the sheets say no rain for 24 hours after application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Check the Temperature</h3>
          <p>
            Temperature matters. Both formulas specify 45-85 degrees for both the air and the surface. A driveway in full sun on a summer afternoon can be well over 85 degrees at the surface even when the air is not, so start early in the morning or work in shade. If the slab has been frozen, Foundation Armor says to let it thaw completely first, or the product may not develop its full protection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Apply With the Right Tool</h3>
          <p>
            Use the right tool. A brush, roller or low-pressure pump sprayer all work, and Foundation Armor caps sprayer pressure at 20 psi and asks for a wet stream rather than a mist. For the solvent version, the sprayer must be acetone-resistant. Apply enough to saturate the surface evenly, then broom out any puddles until they soak in. Puddles left to dry are where problems start: Foundation Armor notes that over-application leaves unreacted material on the surface, which can show as dark or whitish patches. Our page on <a href="/why-is-my-concrete-sealer-turning-white/">why concrete sealer turns white</a> covers how to fix that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Second Coat and Traffic Times</h3>
          <p>
            For the WB, apply the second coat while the first is still wet. For the solvent version, apply it when the surface looks dry, anywhere from 2 to 24 hours later. Keep people off for 12-24 hours and vehicles off for 24-48. Foundation Armor adds that a neutral-pH surface may take several days to reach full water repellency, so do not judge the result with a hose the next morning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compatibility Notes</h3>
          <p>
            Two compatibility notes from the sheets are easy to miss. The SX5000 is not a paint primer, though silicone emulsion paints and many oil-based paints can go over it after testing, with a 72-hour wait specified for the WB. And stucco, plaster and cementitious coatings may not bond over it, so apply those first and let them cure before sealing. Removal is not recommended, which means a mistake is permanent. Test first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reapplication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long It Lasts and When to Reapply
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 10-Year Claim</h3>
          <p>
            Foundation Armor's claim for both SX5000 formulas is up to 10 years of life before a recoat. That is a ceiling, and the real interval depends on traffic, exposure and how well the first application went. A covered porch or a vertical brick wall wears far more slowly than a driveway that sees snow tires, plows and road salt. Our <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-sealer-last/">how long concrete sealer lasts</a> guide compares penetrating and film-forming sealers on this point.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Bead Test</h3>
          <p>
            Do not reseal on a calendar. Test the surface instead. Pour or spray a little water on the concrete in a few places, especially in tire paths and where water collects. If it beads and sits on the surface, the treatment is working. If it darkens the concrete and soaks in within a minute or so, the treatment has worn in that area. A once-a-year check in the autumn, before de-icing salt starts, is enough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recoating Over a Silane-Siloxane</h3>
          <p>
            When you do recoat, the data sheet allows the SX5000 to go over surfaces previously treated with a silane-siloxane, and Foundation Armor's guidance is to reapply only once the existing treatment has degraded and no longer reduces water absorption. Recoating a surface that still repels water wastes product, because the new coat cannot soak in, and it can leave the surface residue described in the application section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surfaces With an Old Film Sealer</h3>
          <p>
            The other case is a surface with an old film sealer on it, such as an acrylic. A penetrating sealer cannot get through a film. Water beading on an acrylic tells you nothing about the concrete underneath, and the SX5000 will sit on top and either wash off or haze. The old film has to be worn off or stripped before a penetrating sealer can work, and that is a bigger job than the sealing itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sx5000-vs-sc25-vs-ar350" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. SX5000 vs SC25 vs AR350: Picking Within Foundation Armor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Foundation Armor sells several sealers that turn up in the same search results, and they do very different jobs. The three that get compared most often are the SX5000 line, the SC25 and the AR350.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The SC25: A One-Coat Siliconate</h3>
          <p>
            The SC25 is a siliconate, not a silane-siloxane. It is water-based, applied in one coat, and Foundation Armor's data sheets list 20% solids and around 175 square feet per gallon on porous concrete. It also reduces water absorption, but siliconates work closer to the surface and are best suited to plain concrete. The SX5000 data sheets list pavers, brick, aggregate and natural stone among their suggested applications, and only the SX5000 sheets carry the up-to-10-year claim. For a plain garage slab or basement floor where the goal is simply less water absorption, the SC25 is reasonable. For exterior concrete that sees salt, spend the extra on the SX5000.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The AR350: An Acrylic Film</h3>
          <p>
            The AR350 is an acrylic. It forms a film on top, adds a wet look and darkens color, and wears faster than a penetrating sealer, typically needing recoats every few years. It is the pick for stamped or exposed-aggregate concrete where the look is the point. It is not a substitute for the SX5000, and the two cannot be layered freely: an acrylic over a fresh silane-siloxane may not bond, and a silane-siloxane cannot penetrate an acrylic. Choose one approach per surface.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
