        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Not Buy the SX5000
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Want a Visible Finish</h3>
          <p>
            Skip it if you want a visible finish. A silane-siloxane leaves concrete looking unsealed, which is a selling point for some buyers and a disappointment for others. People who expected their pavers to come out darker and glossier are among the unhappiest SX5000 owners. If the look matters, buy an acrylic or a wet-look paver sealer and accept the shorter recoat interval.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Oil Is the Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Skip it if oil is the problem. A garage floor or a driveway parking spot with recurring oil drips needs either an oil-repellent sealer, a coating such as epoxy, or regular cleaning. The SX5000 will keep water and salt out of that concrete, but motor oil will still soak in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If Water Is Actively Leaking In</h3>
          <p>
            Skip it for active leaks. If water is coming through a basement wall or floor through cracks, joints or a failed cove joint, a penetrating water repellent will not stop it; Foundation Armor's own sheet says the sealer will not keep water out of cracks, defects or open joints. Fix the drainage and the cracks first, then seal if you still need to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If the Surface Is Not Mineral</h3>
          <p>
            And skip it for surfaces that are not mineral. Silane-siloxane reacts with masonry. It does nothing useful on asphalt, painted concrete, or concrete under an intact film sealer. For everyone else, which is most people with a bare concrete driveway, walk, patio or pool deck in a climate with freezing winters, the SX5000 WB in the right size is one of the best-value treatments you can put down, and the solvent version is there for those who want more solids and can handle it safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between SX5000 and SX5000 WB?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are Foundation Armor silane-siloxane water repellents with the same coverage table and the same up-to-10-year claim. The SX5000 is solvent-based with 40% solids, a 2-24 hour recoat and xylene clean-up. The SX5000 WB is water-based with about 20% solids, a wet-on-wet second coat and soap-and-water clean-up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much area does a 5-gallon pail of SX5000 cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foundation Armor rates both formulas at 125-175 square feet per gallon per coat on porous concrete and 250-350 on dense, trowel-finished concrete. A 5-gallon pail therefore covers about 625-875 square feet of broom-finished concrete in one coat, or roughly half that in the two coats most users apply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Foundation Armor SX5000 change the look of concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foundation Armor describes the dry appearance as invisible and says the sealer maintains the natural, unsealed look of the surface. Some materials darken briefly while wet. It adds no gloss or color enhancement, so test a small patch on pavers or colored concrete before doing the whole area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does SX5000 protect against oil stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Silane-siloxane chemistry repels water, not oil, so motor oil and grease will still soak into sealed concrete. Water-based spills absorb less and clean up more easily. For a garage floor or parking pad with regular oil drips, use an oil-repellent sealer or a coating, or plan on cleaning spills promptly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Foundation Armor SX5000 last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foundation Armor claims up to 10 years before a recoat for both formulas. That is a maximum; driveways with heavy traffic and road salt wear faster than covered porches or vertical walls. Test once a year by wetting the surface. Reapply only where water soaks in rather than beading.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply SX5000 with a pump sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Foundation Armor allows a brush, roller or low-pressure sprayer at no more than 20 psi, set to a wet stream rather than a mist. The solvent SX5000 needs an acetone-resistant sprayer, because ordinary seals can fail in solvent. Broom out any puddles after spraying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the SX5000 better than the SC25?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are different chemistries. The SX5000 is a silane-siloxane that Foundation Armor positions for concrete, pavers, brick and stone with up to 10 years between coats. The SC25 is a water-based siliconate applied in one coat and best suited to plain concrete. For salt-exposed exterior concrete, the SX5000 is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing damp concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foundation Armor says excess moisture inhibits penetration and shortens the treatment's life. After pressure washing, wait until the whole slab is uniformly dry with no dark patches, and only apply when no rain is forecast for 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving puddles to dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Saturate the surface, then broom out puddles until they soak in. Over-applied material that dries on top does not react and can leave dark or whitish blotches, and Foundation Armor does not recommend removal once it has penetrated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the solvent version indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The solvent SX5000 is highly flammable and requires an approved respirator, according to its data sheet. In basements, attached garages and any enclosed space, use the WB instead, which cleans up with soap and water and carries no flammability warning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a wet look or oil protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The SX5000 leaves concrete looking unsealed and repels water, not oil. If you want color enhancement, choose an acrylic. If oil drips are the issue, plan on an oil-repellent product, a coating or regular cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying over an existing film sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating sealer must reach bare concrete. Over an acrylic or other film it cannot soak in and will haze or wash off. Strip or wear off the old film first, and test a small patch to confirm water absorbs before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

